On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:08:02 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> This means that the config that creates the boot image should be
> complete with all the network config etc, so that it can boot with no
> monitor.

infra/hosts/devbsd/devbsd.img.do already builds most of this: a GPT
disk with an EFI partition and a ZFS root from pkgbase, plus a dist/
tree laid down by mtree. I would copy it to infra/hosts/yolo and test
the same image file under bhyve, as the devbsd README describes. Three
things make it boot headless on unknown hardware:

- ifconfig_DEFAULT="DHCP" in rc.conf, so the NIC name does not matter
  (vtnet0 in bhyve, something else on the box). rc.conf(5) documents
  it as the setting "for all interfaces with no ifconfig_<interface>
  variable". A DHCP reservation on the router then fixes the address.
- growfs_enable="YES", so the 1.3G image grows to fill the real disk
  on first boot. /etc/rc.d/growfs handles a ZFS root (lines 117-120
  find the pool's vdev).
- zfs_reguid="YES", already set on devbsd for the makefs guid bug,
  plus sshd and your authorized_keys baked into dist/.

Host keys are generated on first boot, so you would accept a new key
the first time you ssh in. If that matters, we can generate them at
build time and keep them out of jj.

> The plan is to give coding agents root privileges on the host. It's
> considered a completely untrusted host, the only output of it is patches
> that I review.

This is the decision everything else hangs on, and I recommend
against it: give the agents root inside a jail, and keep host root for
you. My reasoning is in the next two answers. In short, securelevel
can stop root from changing the running kernel's state, but I could
not find a way for it to stop host root from changing what boots next,
and root can always reboot.

> 1. Use security levels to prevent system files from changing after it
>    runs.

security(7) gives the levels. At 1, schg and sappnd flags cannot be
cleared, mounted disks cannot be opened for writing, and kld cannot
load or unload. At 2, no disk can be opened for writing "except by
mount(2)". At 3, pf and ipfw rules cannot be changed.

The gap is that "except by mount(2)". The following is inference, from
the man page, not tested: host root at securelevel 2 can still mount
the EFI partition, which is msdosfs and cannot hold schg, and replace
loader.efi. It can also create a new ZFS dataset and point the pool's
bootfs at it, which never touches a flagged file. Either way the next
boot runs root's code before rc raises the securelevel. A test in the
bhyve VM would confirm or refute both.

> 2. The reboot -r mechanism, where it boots to a userland basically
>    running nothing, and then reboots into another userland with all the
>    services (could be combined with 1).

reboot(8) says reboot -r "change[s] the root filesystem while
preserving kernel state", and needs tmpfs loaded. I expect the
securelevel and the loaded pf rules to be part of that state, so a
trusted first stage could load pf, raise the level to 3, and re-root
into the agents' userland. That is inference; I have no kernel source
in this jail to check it. It has the same hole as above, though: the
second stage's root can reboot fully, and the first stage is only as
trusted as its disk.

> I have pf on my firewall, but I would like to have pf on the device
> block output as well. I don't know if that's feasible assuming that it
> has root - but it seems plausible to me that secure level could also
> affect pf, so it's possible to create pf rules that are loaded on boot
> but can't be changed after the fact.

Yes, that is securelevel 3 per security(7). With agents in jails it
also stops mattering whether the agent can reach pf. jail root has no
pf device unless the devfs ruleset exposes it, and our defaults use
ruleset 4. That ruleset's contents are inference; I cannot read
/etc/defaults/devfs.rules from this jail, so check with
"devfs rule -s 4 show" on the host.

For pf to tell agents apart, each jail needs its own address. Today
lab-claude uses ip4 = inherit, so its traffic looks like the host's. A
per-run lo1 alias with NAT, or a vnet jail on an epair, gives pf a
source address to filter on. The egress list would be short:
api.anthropic.com, the pkg mirror if agents install packages, and
nothing else. Patches leave by you pulling, not by the box pushing
(below).

I would still raise the host to securelevel 3 once rc is done
(kern_securelevel_enable and kern_securelevel=3 in rc.conf), as a
second wall behind the jail. Its cost: every kernel module the box
needs (pf, nullfs, tmpfs, linux, vmm) must load at boot, and any pf
change means a reboot.

> I know that rsync.net provides VMs with root on them, and
> they do some locking down, I just don't know what it is exactly.

I do not know either, and have not looked it up. The fact that they
sell a VM suggests their boundary is the hypervisor, not the guest's
securelevel. That points at the other option: bhyve guests on the
box, for work that needs a real kernel, such as booting a
freebsd-src build. Jails are cheaper and fit ZFS clones directly, so
I would start with jails and add bhyve only for that case.

> Also need to think about general architecture of the running system, and
> how it interacts with repos. I want it to operate on lab, or
> freebsd-src, or freebsd-ports, or any number of repos. I want there to
> be isolation across concurrent agents. I'm thinking of checking out the
> repos to zfs datasets, and then cloning those datasets into place in
> jails that actually run claude. I might even use nullmount so they're
> read only.

I agree with clones. A clone of a snapshot is writable, private to
the run, and costs nothing until written, so each run gets its own
working copy of freebsd-src without a checkout. A read-only nullfs
mount fits less well, because jj writes to the repo on every command
(the working-copy snapshot and the op log), so the agent could not
commit.

The layout I have in mind:

- zroot/yolo/repos/lab and zroot/yolo/repos/freebsd-src, kept
  current by a host job
- zroot/yolo/claude, your logged-in claude config
- zroot/yolo/runs/<id>/root, a clone of a jail template
- zroot/yolo/runs/<id>/lab, a clone of repos/lab@<snap>
- zroot/yolo/runs/<id>/claude, a clone of claude@<snap>

Output goes into a per-run outbox, such as a bare git repo in the run
dataset that the agent pushes to. You fetch from it over ssh from
beastie. Then the box holds no credential for anything but the
Anthropic API, and nothing on it can push anywhere.

> I think there still needs to be a zfs dataset that gets cloned, which is
> for the claude config, because multiple agents sharing it will want to
> modify the settings.

Yes. CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR moves .claude.json, the credentials, history
and plugins into one directory, as found in "Re: jails: mount the
claude dir and config file" [2]. lab-claude does that now, from
"[PATCH 0/2] jails: Persist lab-claude's Claude state" [1]. That
mount is read-write because claude writes .claude.json as it runs,
so a read-only mount would fail. A clone per run lets it write, and
destroying the clone throws the writes away, so every run starts from
the same config.

One caveat: the credentials are in that clone, and the agent has root
in its jail, so it can read them. The pf egress list limits where it
could send them, not whether it can read them. An API key scoped to
this box, which you can revoke, keeps that cost bounded.

> Thinking through this flow a bit, I want deterministic tools that will
> launch a jail with whatever repos it needs, perform the work, and clean
> up after itself. Every prompt should be done in a clean, repetable environment.

A sketch, in the shape of lab-process-mail:

  yolo-run -r lab -r freebsd-ports -p prompt.md

1. Snapshot each named repo dataset and the claude dataset.
2. Clone them and the jail template under zroot/yolo/runs/<id>.
3. Start the jail with jail -c, its own address, securelevel 3, and
   the clones mounted.
4. jexec claude -p < prompt.md > /dev/null.
5. Decide success from the outbox: did a new ref land? Never from
   claude's output, per "Own the loop" in CLAUDE.md.
6. jail -r, then zfs destroy -r the run, keeping the outbox.

"[PATCH 1/2] lazy-jail: Initial spec" [3] already covers start, stop
and jail metadata, and its report [4] leaves open whether stop
destroys the root. yolo-run would answer that with yes, so it may be
best built on lazy-jail rather than beside it.

If you agree with the jail-root model, I would next post a spec for
the image (infra/hosts/yolo) and one for yolo-run, as separate
series, so each can be built and tested in bhyve on its own.

